The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = dev_pm_opp_get_opp_count(cpu_dev);
if (ret < 0)
    return -EPROBE_DEFER;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the function `exynos_asv_probe` attempts to gather resources (specifically, the OPP count) for the CPU device before confirming if the SoC is supported. If the SoC is not supported, this leads to an endless deferred probe, as the function will keep returning `-EPROBE_DEFER` without ever successfully completing the probe.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
switch (product_id & EXYNOS_MASK) {
case 0xE5422000:
    probe_func = exynos5422_asv_init;
    break;
default:
    return -ENODEV;
}
```
Reason:
The fix involves checking the `product_id` to determine if the SoC is supported before attempting to gather resources. If the SoC is not supported (i.e., the `product_id` does not match the expected value), the function returns `-ENODEV`, preventing any further resource gathering and thus avoiding the endless deferred probe issue.

